\description{
Tests whether two distance matrices are correlated: for instance, whether
samples with more different communities are also further apart, or more
different in pH. Gives the same statistic as \code{vegan::mantel()} (Pearson,
Spearman, or Kendall correlation), and as \code{vegan::mantel.partial()} with
\code{given}, with permutation p-values. Computes them in parallel C,
straight from the distances, without the \eqn{n \times n} matrices that
\code{mantel()} builds for every permutation.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

The Mantel test (Mantel 1967; Legendre & Legendre 2012) correlates the
\eqn{n(n-1)/2} distances of \code{dm} with the matching distances of \code{y}. The
distances aren't independent of one another, so the p-value comes from
permutations of the samples: the rows and columns of \code{dm} are shuffled
together, \code{y} is held fixed, and the correlation is recomputed. The
p-value is the proportion of permutations, counting the observed one,
with a correlation at least as extreme:
\eqn{(1 + \#\{r_{perm} \ge r\}) / (1 + permutations)} for
\code{alternative = 'greater'}. As in \code{vegan::mantel()}, permuted statistics
within \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} of the observed one count as ties.

The partial Mantel test (Smouse et al. 1986) correlates \code{dm} and \code{y}
after removing the effect of \code{given}, as the partial correlation
\deqn{r_{xy.z} = \frac{r_{xy} - r_{xz} r_{yz}}{\sqrt{1 - r_{xz}^2}
  \sqrt{1 - r_{yz}^2}}}
with the samples of \code{dm} permuted, as in \code{vegan::mantel.partial()}. Its
p-values can be too small when the distances are spatially autocorrelated
(Legendre & Fortin 2010; Guillot & Rousset 2013).

\strong{Speed and memory}

\code{dm} and \code{y} are centred and scaled once (after ranking them, for
Spearman), so each permuted correlation is one pass over the distances,
reading \code{dm} through the permutation: \eqn{O(n^2)} per permutation, with
no \eqn{n \times n} matrix. Kendall's tau-b is counted with Knight's (1966)
merge sort, in \eqn{O(n^2 \log n)} per permutation, where
\code{stats::cor()} takes \eqn{O(n^4)}. Permutations run in parallel.

\strong{Reproducibility}

The permutations come from \code{seed}, not from R's random number generator,
so \code{set.seed()} has no effect, and each permutation has a random number
stream of its own. P-values are identical from run to run and for any
number of \code{cpus}. The random permutations differ from \code{vegan::mantel()}'s,
so p-values agree with it only up to sampling error, unless the same
\code{permutations} matrix is given to both.

\strong{Differences from vegan::mantel()}
\itemize{
\item One function does both the simple and the partial test.
\item \code{y} and \code{given} may also be numeric vectors, one value per sample.
\item Samples are matched by name between \code{dm}, \code{y}, \code{given}, and \code{strata}.
\item \code{alternative} adds lower-tail and two-sided tests.
\item There's no \code{na.rm}: distances must not be \code{NA}.
\item When there are fewer possible permutations than requested,
\code{vegan::mantel()} (via \code{permute}) runs each of them once instead.
\code{mantel()} always draws random permutations.
}
